Below is a practical, step-by-step guide for moving a Mapbox-GL-JS app that uses **`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw`** (the drawing UI) and **`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`** (the search box) over to **MapLibre GL JS**.  
It covers:

| Library | What changes are required? | Why? | Quick migration checklist |
|---------|----------------------------|------|----------------------------|
| **`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw`** | ✅ No code-change in most cases – just install the same package and point it at a `maplibre-gl` map instance. <br>🛠️ If you hit a peer-dependency warning, install the **`maplibre-gl`** version that satisfies the plugin’s `peerDependencies`. | The draw plugin only talks to the **Mapbox GL JS v1** API (the same public API that MapLibre implements). It does **not** depend on any Mapbox-hosted services. | 1. `npm i maplibre-gl @mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw`  <br>2. Replace `import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl'` → `import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'`  <br>3. Pass the `maplibregl.Map` instance to `new MapboxDraw(...)`.  <br>4. (Optional) If you use TypeScript, add `/// <reference types="maplibre-gl" />` or install `@types/maplibre-gl`. |
| **`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`** | ❗️ The original **Mapbox** geocoder plugin is tightly coupled to the Mapbox Geocoding API (requires a Mapbox access token). <br>✅ You have three viable paths: <br>1️⃣ Keep using the same plugin **if you still want to call Mapbox’s geocoding service** (you can, but you’ll still need a Mapbox token). <br>2️⃣ Switch to the **community-maintained fork** `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder` which works with any generic geocoder endpoint (Mapbox, Pelias, Photon, Algolia, etc.). <br>3️⃣ Replace it entirely with another open-source geocoder UI (e.g., **`leaflet-geosearch`**, **`geocoder-gl`**, **`react-map-gl-geocoder`**, etc.). | The original package ships with a hard-coded Mapbox API URL and a default token handling flow. When you move off Mapbox-hosted tiles you usually also want to move off Mapbox’s geocoding service (or at least make the dependency explicit). The fork removes the Mapbox-only assumptions and lets you plug any endpoint. | **If you stay on Mapbox Geocoding** (quick-fix): <br>1. Keep `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`. <br>2. Add a Mapbox token to the `geocoder` options (`accessToken: '<your-token>'`). <br>3. No other code changes. <br><br>**If you want a non-Mapbox service** (recommended for a fully open-source stack): <br>1. `npm i @maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder`  <br>2. Replace imports: `import MapboxGeocoder from '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder'` → `import MapLibreGeocoder from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder'`. <br>3. Provide a `geocoder` option that points to your chosen endpoint, e.g.: <br>```js\nconst geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({\n  // any of the supported services – see docs for the exact shape\n  forwardGeocode: async (query) => {\n    const response = await fetch(`https://photon.komoot.io/api/?q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`);\n    const data = await response.json();\n    return {\n      features: data.features.map(f => ({\n        type: 'Feature',\n        geometry: f.geometry,\n        properties: { title: f.properties.name, description: f.properties.city },\n        place_name: f.properties.name,\n        center: f.geometry.coordinates,\n        place_type: ['address']\n      }))\n    };\n  },\n  placeholder: 'Search…',\n  maplibregl: maplibregl // required for the fork\n});\n``` <br>4. Add the control to the map: `map.addControl(geocoder);` <br>5. If you use CSS, import the fork’s stylesheet (`@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder/dist/maplibre-gl-geocoder.css`). |

---

## 1️⃣ Detailed migration for **`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw`**

### Why it works out-of-the-box
* The draw plugin only uses the **public Mapbox GL JS v1 API** (`addSource`, `addLayer`, `on`, `off`, `queryRenderedFeatures`, etc.).  
* MapLibre GL JS is a **drop-in replacement** for that API (it even re-exports the same class names).  

### Typical code before migration

```js
import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl';
import MapboxDraw from '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw';
import '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw/dist/mapbox-gl-draw.css';

mapboxgl.accessToken = 'pk.ey...';
const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v11',
  center: [-122.45, 37.78],
  zoom: 12
});

const draw = new MapboxDraw({
  displayControlsDefault: false,
  controls: { polygon: true, trash: true }
});
map.addControl(draw);
```

### After migration

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import MapboxDraw from '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw';
import '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw/dist/mapbox-gl-draw.css';

// No access token needed for MapLibre unless you still call Mapbox services.
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any public style
  center: [-122.45, 37.78],
  zoom: 12
});

const draw = new MapboxDraw({
  displayControlsDefault: false,
  controls: { polygon: true, trash: true }
});
map.addControl(draw);
```

### Gotchas & tips

| Issue | Fix |
|-------|-----|
| **Peer-dependency warning** (`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw` expects `mapbox-gl@^1.13.0`) | The warning is harmless – MapLibre implements the same API. If you want a clean install, add a **`resolutions`** entry (yarn) or **`overrides`** (npm) to force `mapbox-gl` to resolve to `maplibre-gl`. Example (npm):<br>`"overrides": { "mapbox-gl": "npm:maplibre-gl@^2.4.0" }` |
| **TypeScript** complains about missing `mapboxgl` types | Install `@types/maplibre-gl` (`npm i -D @types/maplibre-gl`) and add a module alias if you import as `mapboxgl`. Or just change the import name to `maplibregl` and update the types accordingly. |
| **CSS** path unchanged | The CSS file lives in the same package, so you can keep the import line exactly as shown. |
| **Custom draw styles** that reference `mapbox://` sources | Replace those with URLs that MapLibre can resolve (e.g., `https://.../tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf` or a local vector source). |

---

## 2️⃣ Detailed migration for **`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`**

### Two main scenarios

| Scenario | What you keep / change |
|----------|------------------------|
| **A. Keep using Mapbox Geocoding** (fastest path) | • Keep the original `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder` package.<br>• Provide a valid Mapbox access token in the geocoder options.<br>• No changes to the UI or CSS. |
| **B. Switch to an open-source geocoder** (recommended for a fully open stack) | • Replace the package with the community fork `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder`.<br>• Point the geocoder to any public endpoint (Pelias, Photon, Algolia, OpenCage, etc.).<br>• Update import statements and CSS path. |

Below are the concrete steps for each.

---

### **Scenario A – Keep Mapbox Geocoding**

```bash
npm i @mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder
```

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import MapboxGeocoder from '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder';
import '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder/dist/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({ /* … */ });

const geocoder = new MapboxGeocoder({
  accessToken: 'pk.eyYOUR_MAPBOX_TOKEN', // <-- still required
  maplibregl: maplibregl,                // tell the plugin which GL lib you use
  placeholder: 'Search places…',
  // optional: limit results, bbox, etc.
});

map.addControl(geocoder);
```

**What you lose:** you remain dependent on Mapbox’s hosted geocoding service (and its usage limits / pricing). If that’s fine for your project, this is the quickest migration.

---

### **Scenario B – Use the MapLibre-maintained fork (or any other service)**

#### 2.1 Install the fork

```bash
npm i @maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder
```

> The fork is a **drop-in replacement** for the original plugin, but it **does not embed a Mapbox-only endpoint**. You must supply a `forwardGeocode` (and optionally `reverseGeocode`) function that talks to whatever service you prefer.

#### 2.2 Basic usage with the **Photon** public endpoint (free, open-source)

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import MapLibreGeocoder from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder';
import '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder/dist/maplibre-gl-geocoder.css';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
  center: [-122.45, 37.78],
  zoom: 12
});

const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  // The `forwardGeocode` signature matches the Mapbox plugin's expectations.
  forwardGeocode: async (query) => {
    const response = await fetch(
      `https://photon.komoot.io/api/?q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}&limit=5`
    );
    const data = await response.json();

    // Convert Photon’s response to the GeoJSON FeatureCollection format
    // expected by the control.
    return {
      features: data.features.map((f) => ({
        type: 'Feature',
        geometry: f.geometry,
        properties: {
          // `title` is what the UI shows in the dropdown.
          title: f.properties.name,
          // `description` appears as a secondary line (optional).
          description: `${f.properties.city || ''} ${f.properties.country || ''}`.trim()
        },
        // `place_name` is used for the input value after selection.
        place_name: f.properties.name,
        // `center` is required for the map to fly to the result.
        center: f.geometry.coordinates,
        // `place_type` can be any array; `address` works well.
        place_type: ['address']
      }))
    };
  },

  // Optional: you can also implement `reverseGeocode` if you need it.
  // reverseGeocode: async (req) => { … },

  placeholder: 'Search places…',
  maplibregl: maplibregl, // tells the control which GL lib you use
  // You can also pass `zoom: 14` or `flyTo: { speed: 1.2 }` etc.
});

map.addControl(geocoder);
```

