### TL;DR  
`map.loadImage` in **MapLibre GL v2+** no longer uses the old callback-style API that Mapbox GL did.  
When you pass a callback it is silently ignored, so the image never gets added and your custom marker icons disappear.

**Fix** – use the Promise-based version (or `await` it) **after the map’s style has loaded**.  

```js
// Make sure the style is ready
map.on('load', async () => {
  try {
    // 1️⃣ Load the image – returns a Promise in MapLibre
    const img = await map.loadImage('https://my.cdn.com/pin.png');

    // 2️⃣ Add it to the style (you can also pass options like {sdf: true})
    map.addImage('pin', img);

    // 3️⃣ Now you can use the image in a Symbol layer or a custom Marker
    new maplibregl.Marker({ element: createPinElement() })
      .setLngLat([lng, lat])
      .addTo(map);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('❌ loadImage failed:', err);
  }
});
```

If you prefer the classic callback style, you can still use it **only on MapLibre GL v1.x**. In v2+ the callback is deprecated and will never be invoked, which is exactly why you saw the “callback not being called” message.

---

## Why the old code stopped working

| Mapbox GL (≤ v2) | MapLibre GL (v2+) |
|-----------------|-------------------|
| `map.loadImage(url, cb)` – callback is called with `(err, image)` | **Callback is ignored** – the method now returns a `Promise` that resolves with an `ImageBitmap` (or rejects with an error). |
| `map.addImage(name, image)` works with an `HTMLImageElement` or `ImageBitmap` | Same, but you must actually get the image from the Promise first. |

Because the callback never fires, `map.addImage('pin', img)` never runs, so any layer that references `'pin'` (or any custom `Marker` that uses `map.addImage`) ends up with a missing icon.

---

## Step-by-step migration checklist

1. **Upgrade imports**  
   ```js
   // Old
   import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl';
   // New
   import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
   ```

2. **Replace every `loadImage(..., cb)` with the Promise version**  
   ```js
   // Old
   map.loadImage(url, (err, img) => { … });

   // New (async/await)
   const img = await map.loadImage(url);
   map.addImage('pin', img);
   ```

   Or with `.then()` if you don’t want async/await:  
   ```js
   map.loadImage(url)
       .then(img => map.addImage('pin', img))
       .catch(err => console.error(err));
   ```

3. **Make sure the map’s style is loaded before you call `loadImage`**  
   ```js
   map.on('load', () => { /* load images here */ });
   ```

4. **CORS considerations**  
   * If the image lives on a different origin, the server must send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or your domain).  
   * If you can’t change the server, host the PNG/SVG on the same origin or embed it as a data-URL.

5. **Optional: set `pixelRatio` or `sdf` options**  
   ```js
   map.addImage('pin', img, { pixelRatio: 2, sdf: true });
   ```

6. **Test** – open the console, you should see no “callback not being called” warning and the marker icons should appear.

---

## Quick sanity-check code snippet

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – custom pin</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map { width:100%; height:100vh; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js';

  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style:    'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center:   [0, 0],
    zoom:     2
  });

  map.on('load', async () => {
    try {
      const img = await map.loadImage('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/maplibre/maplibre-gl-js/main/docs/assets/pin.png');
      map.addImage('my-pin', img);

      // Use the image in a Symbol layer
      map.addLayer({
        id: 'points',
        type: 'symbol',
        source: {
          type: 'geojson',
          data: {
            type: 'FeatureCollection',
            features: [{ type: 'Feature', geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [0, 0] } }]
          }
        },
        layout: {
          'icon-image': 'my-pin',
          'icon-size': 0.5
        }
      });
    } catch (e) {
      console.error('Failed to load pin image', e);
    }
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Run that in a browser – you’ll see a pin at the map centre. If it works, the same pattern will fix your app.

---

### Bottom line
Your code isn’t “wrong” for Mapbox GL, but MapLibre GL v2+ changed `loadImage` to a **Promise-based API**. Switch to `await map.loadImage(...)` (or `.then`) after the map’s `load` event, and the icons will reappear. 🎉

Below is a quick-reference guide to every practical way you can drop a **geocoder search box** onto a **Mapbox GL JS v2** map.  
I’ve grouped the options by **how much you rely on Mapbox’s own services** vs. **third-party / open-source services**, and I’ve added code snippets, pros/cons, and a short “when to use it” note for each.

---

## 1️⃣ The “official” way – **Mapbox GL Geocoder** (the `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder` plugin)

| Feature | Details |
|---------|---------|
| **Package** | `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder` (v5.x works with Mapbox GL JS v2) |
| **Installation** | <ul><li>npm/yarn: `npm i @mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`</li><li>CDN: `<script src="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/plugins/mapbox-gl-geocoder/v5.0.1/mapbox-gl-geocoder.min.js"></script>` and CSS `<link href="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/plugins/mapbox-gl-geocoder/v5.0.1/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css" rel="stylesheet" />`</li></ul> |
| **How it works** | UI component that calls the **Mapbox Geocoding API** under the hood (requires a Mapbox access token). |
| **Customization** | • Placeholder text, language, country, types, limit, proximity, bbox, etc.<br>• CSS can be overridden (the control is just a `<div>` with class `mapboxgl-ctrl-geocoder`).<br>• You can listen to events (`result`, `clear`, `loading`, `error`). |
| **Pros** | • Tight integration – the result is automatically added to the map as a `Marker` (or you can handle it yourself).<br>• Handles rate-limit back-off, pagination, and fuzzy matching for you.<br>• Works out-of-the-box with Mapbox GL JS v2 (no extra polyfills). |
| **Cons** | • Tied to Mapbox’s **Geocoding API** → you need a Mapbox token and you’ll be billed per request (free tier: 100 k/month).<br>• Not open-source data – you can’t swap the backend without writing your own wrapper. |
| **When to use** | You already have a Mapbox token, you want the fastest integration, and you’re happy to pay for Mapbox’s geocoding service. |
| **Minimal example** | ```js
import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl';
import MapboxGeocoder from '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder';

// 1️⃣ Initialise the map
mapboxgl.accessToken = 'YOUR_MAPBOX_TOKEN';
const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12',
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
  zoom: 12
});

// 2️⃣ Add the geocoder control
const geocoder = new MapboxGeocoder({
  accessToken: mapboxgl.accessToken,
  mapboxgl: mapboxgl,          // required to use the built-in marker
  placeholder: 'Search places…',
  proximity: { longitude: -122.447303, latitude: 37.753574 }, // optional bias
  countries: 'us',            // optional filter
  limit: 5
});

map.addControl(geocoder, 'top-left');

// 3️⃣ (Optional) React to a result
geocoder.on('result', e => {
  console.log('Selected feature:', e.result);
});
``` |
| **Docs** | https://github.com/mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder |

---

## 2️⃣ Build your own UI but still use **Mapbox Geocoding API** (fetch + custom HTML)

If you want a completely custom look (e.g., a modal, a sidebar, or a React component) you can call the API yourself and then feed the result back into the map.

| Feature | Details |
|---------|---------|
| **Endpoint** | `https://api.mapbox.com/geocoding/v5/mapbox.places/<query>.json?access_token=YOUR_TOKEN&...` |
| **Typical stack** | Any UI framework (React, Vue, Svelte, plain HTML) + `fetch`/`axios`. |
| **Pros** | • Full control over UI/UX (autocomplete dropdowns, debouncing, custom icons).<br>• You can combine the results with other data sources (e.g., local POI list). |
| **Cons** | • You have to implement debouncing, pagination, error handling, and rate-limit back-off yourself.<br>• No built-in marker handling – you must add it manually. |
| **When to use** | You need a UI that looks *exactly* like your brand, or you want to merge Mapbox results with another source. |
| **Skeleton code** | ```js
const token = 'YOUR_MAPBOX_TOKEN';
const input = document.getElementById('search-input');
const resultsDiv = document.getElementById('search-results');

let abortController = null;

input.addEventListener('input', e => {
  const query = e.target.value.trim();
  if (!query) { resultsDiv.innerHTML = ''; return; }

  // Debounce (simple 300 ms)
  clearTimeout(input._debounce);
  input._debounce = setTimeout(() => {
    // Cancel previous request
    if (abortController) abortController.abort();
    abortController = new AbortController();

    fetch(`https://api.mapbox.com/geocoding/v5/mapbox.places/${encodeURIComponent(query)}.json?access_token=${token}&autocomplete=true&limit=5`, {
      signal: abortController.signal
    })
      .then(r => r.json())
      .then(data => {
        resultsDiv.innerHTML = data.features
          .map(f => `<div class="result" data-coords='${JSON.stringify(f.center)}'>${f.place_name}</div>`)
          .join('');
      })
      .catch(err => {
        if (err.name !== 'AbortError') console.error(err);
      });
  }, 300);
});

resultsDiv.addEventListener('click', e => {
  const el = e.target.closest('.result');
  if (!el) return;
  const coords = JSON.parse(el.dataset.coords);
  // Fly to the result
  map.flyTo({ center: coords, zoom: 14 });
  // Optionally add a marker
  new mapboxgl.Marker().setLngLat(coords).addTo(map);
});
``` |
| **Tip** | Use the `autocomplete=true` flag for “type-ahead” results and `proximity` to bias results toward the current map view. |

---

## 3️⃣ Open-source alternatives that **don’t require a Mapbox token**

If you want to stay completely free of Mapbox’s usage limits (or you’re building a product that can’t expose a Mapbox token), you can swap the backend for an open geocoder and keep the UI layer either as a **drop-in replacement** or a **custom component**.

| Option | Service | How to plug into Mapbox GL JS v2 | Key notes |
|--------|---------|--------------------------------|-----------|
| **MapLibre GL Geocoder** | Open-source UI that works with **any** geocoding endpoint (Mapbox, Pelias, Photon, etc.) | `npm i @maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder` (or use the same `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder` UI but point it at a different endpoint via the `geocoder` option). | The UI is identical to Mapbox’s, but you provide your own `fetch` function. |
| **Pelias** | Open-source geocoder (hosted by many providers: Mapzen, GeoNames, etc.) | Use the same UI as above, but set `url: 'https://api.geocode.earth/v1/search'` (or your own Pelias instance). | Free tier on geocode.earth (requires API key). |
| **Photon** | OpenStreetMap-based geocoder (hosted by `photon.komoot.io` or self-hosted). | Same UI, `url: 'https://photon.komoot.io/api/'`. | No API key needed, but rate-limits are low; best for low-traffic demos. |
| **OpenCage** | Commercial service that aggregates OSM, OpenStreetMap, and other data. | UI → `url: 'https://api.opencagedata.com/geocode/v1/json'` + `params: { key: 'YOUR_KEY' }`. | 2 500 free requests/day; good global coverage. |
| **Nominatim** | OSM’s official geocoder (public instance at `https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org`). | UI → `url: 'https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search'` + `params: { format: 'json', addressdetails: 1 }`. | Must respect the usage policy (1 req/s per IP). |
| **Algolia Places** (deprecated but still usable) | Algolia-hosted OSM data. | UI → custom fetch; Algolia provides a ready-made UI component. | Service is being sunset; not recommended for new projects. |

### 3A. Using **MapLibre GL Geocoder** as a drop-in

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';          // works with Mapbox GL JS v2 as well
import MapLibreGeocoder from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder';

// Initialise the map (Mapbox GL JS v2 works the same way)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12',
  center: [-73.985130, 40.758896],
  zoom: 12
});

// Use Photon as the backend
const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  placeholder: 'Search…',
  // Photon endpoint (no API key)
  url: 'https://photon.komoot.io/api/',
  // Mapbox GL JS expects a `mapboxgl` reference for the built-in marker
  mapboxgl: maplibregl,
  // Optional: limit results, bias, etc.
  limit: 5
});

map.addControl(geocoder);
```

> **Why this works:** The geocoder UI is completely UI-agnostic; you just tell it where to send the query (`url`) and how to parse the response (`params`). The control will still emit the same events (`result`, `clear`, …) that you’re used to with the Mapbox plugin.

### 3B. Building a **custom UI** with an open service

If you want a React component that talks to **Nominatim**, you can use the tiny library **`react-nominatim`** or roll your own:

```tsx
import { useState } from 'react';
import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl';

function NominatimSearch({ map }: { map: mapboxgl.Map }) {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [hits, setHits] = useState<any[]>([]);

  const search = async (q: string) => {
    const resp = await fetch(
      `https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?format=json&q=${encodeURIComponent(q)}&addressdetails=1&limit=5`
    );
    const data = await resp.json();
    setHits(data);
  };

  const onSelect = (place: any) => {
    const [lon, lat
